#cloud-config
# nat-node.yaml.tftpl (ansible/cloud-init)
#
# This is the cloud-init user-data template every NAT node boots with. It
# fully configures a node with zero manual post-boot steps: the nftables
# NAT ruleset, the Prometheus exporter, optional conntrackd buddy-pair
# sync, and optional BGP-based buddy IP failover (FRR). Rendered per-node by
# terraform/modules/nat-fleet/main.tf, with that node's own static IPs and
# this pool's feature flags substituted into the `$${...}` / `%%{ if ... }`
# placeholders below (escaped here with an extra $/% so Terraform's
# templatefile() -- which scans the WHOLE file for interpolation syntax,
# including inside # comments -- doesn't try to actually evaluate this
# description of the syntax as the syntax itself).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# Interface layout (see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html §1.3 "The
# Three-Interface Node"):
#
# 1) eth0 (public) - A real public IP, never VPC's 1:1 NAT -- required
#    because VPC IPs cannot use Linode's IP Sharing feature, which buddy
#    IP failover depends on.
# 2) eth1 (VPC)     - Serves buddy-pair conntrackd sync traffic.
# 3) eth2 (VLAN)    - Transparent NAT/masquerade for the private client
#    fleet. VLAN is used instead of VPC here because VPC cannot
#    transit-route a packet through a peer instance to a non-VPC
#    destination (validated empirically -- see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html
#    §1.2 "The Constraint That Shapes Everything").
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# What gets installed, conditionally, based on this pool's flags:
#
# 1) nftables + the Prometheus exporter - Always installed; this is the
#    core NAT/masquerade path and its health/metrics surface.
# 2) conntrackd + buddy-sync             - Installed only when
#    natctl_roster_url is set. Mirrors this node's connection state to a
#    dynamically-assigned buddy elsewhere in the pool. On its own this
#    only protects connection STATE -- it does not help a dead node's
#    public IP become reachable again, which is why IP failover (below)
#    exists as a separate, additive mechanism.
# 3) FRR (BGP-based IP Sharing)           - Installed only when
#    ip_failover_enabled is true. Each node runs two independent,
#    route-map-tagged BGP `network` statements at once -- one for its own
#    IP ("primary", pushed once at boot, self-detected, never touched
#    again) and one for its buddy's IP ("secondary", assigned dynamically
#    by buddy_sync.py as buddy pairings change). This is what makes buddy
#    IP failover bidirectional -- see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html
#    §4.4 "BGP-Based IP Failover" for the live validation this design is
#    built on.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# nftables.conf and the nat-exporter/lng-buddy-sync/conntrackd@/natctl
# systemd unit files are all fetched at boot from Object Storage (curl, in
# runcmd below), not embedded as write_files content: blocks -- fetching
# exporter.py/buddy_sync.py/the natctl package alone is not enough
# headroom against Linode's 16384-byte decoded cloud-init limit once
# natctl_on_node_enabled is on for a real environment. None of these
# specific files carry secrets (verified line-by-line): nftables.conf is
# just NAT/firewall rules; the systemd unit files only reference external
# env files by path, never contain a credential themselves. See
# terraform/modules/artifacts/main.tf's header comment for the static
# (one-per-environment) uploads and terraform/modules/nat-fleet/main.tf for
# the per-node upload (nftables.conf differs per node by IP/CIDR, so it's
# NOT a candidate for the shared artifacts module -- each node gets its own
# object). natctl_config_yaml is
# deliberately NOT moved to Object Storage fetch -- it embeds this pool's
# plaintext root_pass (needed by natctl's own elastic-node provisioning),
# and this module's uploads are public-read (see artifacts/main.tf's "Why
# public-read" section) -- a secret-bearing document has no business on a
# public URL. It stays a small write_files content: block, same as before.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# Usage:
#
# - Never edit this file's rendered output directly on a running node --
#   change the template (or the Terraform variables feeding it) and
#   re-provision instead, so the floor stays reproducible.
# - Keep this file's behavior in sync with controller/natctl/cloud_init.py,
#   which renders the equivalent bootstrap for natctl-provisioned elastic
#   nodes, so a floor node and an elastic node come up with the exact
#   same three-interface + FRR layout regardless of which renderer built
#   them.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# Best Practices:
#
# - Router-id is set exactly once, at boot, and never touched live --
#   changing `bgp router-id` on a running FRR instance forces a full BGP
#   session reset on every peer. Only the "secondary" network statement
#   is ever touched after boot.
# - Order matters in `runcmd` below: FRR/buddy-sync's env file must be
#   fully written (including the self-detected NAT_PUBLIC_IP) before
#   lng-buddy-sync starts, since it reads that file once at process start.
#   Likewise, every fetched systemd unit file must land on disk (and get a
#   `systemctl daemon-reload`) BEFORE the matching `systemctl enable --now`
#   -- unlike write_files content, curl'd units are not on disk yet when
#   cloud-init reaches the write_files stage, only once runcmd actually
#   runs the fetch step.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------
# Author:
# - Sandip Gangdhar
# - GitHub: https://github.com/sandipgangdhar
#
# (c) Linode-NAT-Gateway (LNG) | Developed by Sandip Gangdhar | 2026
# -----------------------------------------------------

hostname: ${node_name}
# `authorized_keys` and `root_pass` are both set on every node (root_pass
# for Lish emergency console access, a separate auth channel from SSH),
# but the base Ubuntu
# image leaves sshd's PasswordAuthentication/PermitRootLogin at their
# password-permitting defaults unless something explicitly overrides
# them — confirmed live, not assumed (`sshd -T` on a real node showed
# `passwordauthentication yes`). ssh_pwauth: false makes cloud-init write
# `PasswordAuthentication no` into sshd_config and restart sshd, so SSH
# itself becomes key-only while Lish's own (unrelated) password auth is
# untouched.
ssh_pwauth: false
package_update: true
package_upgrade: false

packages:
  - nftables
%{ if agent_distribution != "binary" ~}
  - python3
%{ endif ~}
  - curl
  - conntrack
%{ if natctl_roster_url != "" ~}
  - conntrackd
%{ endif ~}
%{ if ip_failover_enabled ~}
  # apt-packaged frr, not a GitHub-released static binary -- no
  # version-pin runcmd step needed.
  - frr
%{ endif ~}
%{ if natctl_on_node_enabled && agent_distribution != "binary" ~}
  # natctl-on-node (opt-in) — this node itself is leader-election-
  # eligible to run the fleet controller, removing the requirement for a
  # separate dedicated control-plane host. See leader_election section
  # further down and controller/natctl/leader_election.py. pip is only
  # needed to install requirements.txt for the .py-source natctl
  # package -- binary mode fetches one executable instead, see runcmd
  # below.
  - python3-pip
%{ endif ~}

write_files:
  - path: /etc/nat-exporter/env
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0640'
    # NAT_PUBLIC_IPS is deliberately absent here and filled in by runcmd
    # below (self-discovered post-boot) rather than passed in as a
    # template variable — this instance's own public IP is only known
    # AFTER Linode creates it, and it's created as part of this same
    # cloud-init payload, so referencing it here would be a dependency
    # cycle in Terraform. See the comment in nftables.conf.tftpl for the
    # same issue on the NAT rule side (solved there with `masquerade`).
    #
    # NAT_PRIVATE_IP is set to the VLAN IP (not VPC) because client-agent —
    # the fast-path, most failure-sensitive consumer of the roster's
    # private_ip field — lives on the VLAN, not the VPC: VPC cannot
    # transit-route a packet through a peer instance to a non-VPC
    # destination (see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html §1.2).
    # NAT_VPC_IP is carried separately.
    content: |
      NAT_NODE_NAME=${node_name}
      NAT_NODE_ID=${node_id}
      NAT_POOL_NAME=${pool_name}
      NAT_PRIVATE_IP=${vlan_ip}
      NAT_VPC_IP=${vpc_ip}
      NAT_PORT_RANGE_MIN=1024
      NAT_PORT_RANGE_MAX=65535

%{ if natctl_on_node_enabled ~}
  # natctl-on-node (opt-in, natctl_on_node_enabled). This node runs the
  # SAME natctl package terraform/modules/observability embeds on its
  # dedicated host — mirrored file-for-file here rather than factored into
  # a shared template, since cloud-init templates can't share includes.
  # Every node in this pool gets the full package + config; only ONE of
  # them (the elected leader — see leader_election.py) is ever allowed to
  # actually mutate Linode state at any given moment, so it's safe for
  # this to be on by default across the whole pool once
  # natctl_on_node_enabled is true.
  #
  # This composed config is the ONE piece of natctl-on-node content that
  # stays embedded (not fetched) -- see this file's header note above for
  # why (it carries this pool's plaintext root_pass, and every other
  # Object Storage upload in this design is public-read).
  - path: /etc/natctl/config.yaml
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0600'
    content: |
      ${indent(6, natctl_config_yaml)}

  - path: /etc/natctl/env
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0600'
    # NATCTL_SELF_NODE_ID is safe to set at Terraform render time (this
    # node's own name is decided BEFORE creation, unlike its Linode
    # instance ID or public IP — see locals.node_ids in main.tf).
    # NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID, LINODE_TOKEN's peers on every other node, and
    # the Object Storage credentials for the leader-election lease are
    # deliberately kept out of /etc/natctl/config.yaml itself (see
    # config.py's LeaderElectionConfig docstring) — this file gets the
    # 0600 treatment instead, same as LINODE_TOKEN always has. Only
    # written when object_storage_access_key/secret_key are actually set,
    # since a fleet can enable natctl_on_node_enabled with leader_election
    # left disabled in natctl_config_yaml (single-mutator-by-convention,
    # no fencing) if it isn't ready for real auto-failover yet.
    content: |
      NATCTL_SELF_NODE_ID=${node_id}
      LINODE_TOKEN=${linode_token}
%{ if object_storage_access_key != "" ~}
      NATCTL_OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY=${object_storage_access_key}
      NATCTL_OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_KEY=${object_storage_secret_key}
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}

  - path: /etc/sysctl.d/99-lng-nat.conf
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0644'
    content: |
      net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
      net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter = 0
      net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter = 0
      net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max = ${conntrack_max}
      net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout = 20
      net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_tcp_timeout_established = 3600

%{ if natctl_roster_url != "" ~}
  # Conntrack buddy-pair sync — see buddy-sync/ and docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html
  # §4.3 "Connection-State Sync". Opt-in: only wired up when natctl_roster_url is set for this pool
  # (terraform/modules/nat-fleet's natctl_roster_url variable). Nodes don't
  # get conntrackd.conf at boot — buddy_sync.py writes it once it learns
  # this node's assigned buddy from natctl, so there's no chicken-and-egg
  # problem with a peer IP that isn't known yet.
  - path: /etc/lng-buddy-sync/env
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0640'
    # NAT_PRIVATE_IP here is the VPC IP (eth1), not VLAN — buddy-pair
    # conntrackd sync traffic runs over VPC in v4 (see the nftables.conf
    # pool_subnet_cidr comment in main.tf), separate from the VLAN-side
    # NAT_PRIVATE_IP used in /etc/nat-exporter/env above. Yes, two files
    # define the same env var name to mean two different IPs on purpose —
    # each is local to its own service's env file, never sourced together.
    # NAT_PUBLIC_IP (only when ip_failover_enabled) is deliberately
    # absent here too — appended by runcmd below, once this node's own
    # public IP is self-discovered post-boot, same reasoning as
    # NAT_PUBLIC_IPS in /etc/nat-exporter/env.
    content: |
      NATCTL_ROSTER_URL=${natctl_roster_url}/fleet/${pool_name}
      NAT_NODE_ID=${node_id}
      NAT_PRIVATE_IP=${vpc_ip}
      NAT_IP_FAILOVER_ENABLED=${ip_failover_enabled}
%{ endif ~}

%{ if ip_failover_enabled ~}
  # BGP-based IP Sharing via FRR lets a buddy
  # take over a dead node's public IP, which is what actually makes the
  # conntrackd sync above useful for connection survival (without this, a
  # dead node's public IP is simply gone and no amount of mirrored
  # conntrack state helps — see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html
  # §4.4 "BGP-Based IP Failover"). FRR, unlike
  # lelastic, can run two independent, route-map-tagged `network`
  # statements on one node at once — one for this node's own IP
  # ("primary"), one for its buddy's ("secondary") — which is what makes
  # BIDIRECTIONAL buddy IP failover possible at all (§4.4's live
  # validation). daemons/frr.conf are installed here; router-id and the
  # "primary" self-announcement are pushed once at boot by runcmd below
  # (self-detected — this node's own public IP isn't known until Linode
  # creates it), and never touched again. Only the "secondary" statement
  # is assigned dynamically, by natctl's ip-failover orchestration +
  # buddy_sync.py, once this node has a buddy — same chicken-and-egg
  # reasoning as buddy_sync.py owning conntrackd's lifecycle above.
  - path: /etc/frr/daemons
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0644'
    content: |
      zebra=yes
      bgpd=yes
      staticd=yes
      vtysh_enable=yes

  - path: /etc/frr/frr.conf
    # owner deliberately left as the write_files default (root:root),
    # NOT frr:frr -- write_files runs before the frr package (which
    # creates that system user) is guaranteed installed, and `owner:
    # frr:frr` here races that install: Akamai's DataSourceAkamaiLocal
    # processes write_files in an early pass that reliably loses this
    # race, permanently failing with `getpwnam(): name not found: 'frr'`
    # and leaving `cloud-init status` stuck reporting error forever, even
    # once boot otherwise completes cleanly. The chown to frr:frr now happens explicitly in
    # runcmd below, after packages are installed, right before frr
    # starts -- required because buddy_sync.py's apply_secondary_ip()
    # does `vtysh ... write memory`, which needs this file writable by
    # the frr user to persist config across restarts.
    permissions: '0640'
    content: |
      ! LNG buddy IP failover — base FRR config.
      ! router-id and the "primary" network statement (this node's own
      ! public IP) are pushed once at boot by runcmd below, not templated
      ! here. The "secondary" statement (buddy's IP) is added/removed live
      ! by buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py. Mirrors controller/natctl/cloud_init.py's
      ! render_frr_conf() — the two must stay behaviorally identical, so an
      ! elastic node comes up with the exact same FRR base config a floor
      ! node gets.
      router bgp 65001
        no bgp ebgp-requires-policy
        coalesce-time 1000
        bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax
        neighbor RS peer-group
        neighbor RS remote-as external
        neighbor RS remote-as 65000
        neighbor RS ebgp-multihop 10
        neighbor RS capability extended-nexthop
        neighbor 2600:3c0f:${linode_bgp_dcid}:34::1 peer-group RS
        neighbor 2600:3c0f:${linode_bgp_dcid}:34::2 peer-group RS
        neighbor 2600:3c0f:${linode_bgp_dcid}:34::3 peer-group RS
        neighbor 2600:3c0f:${linode_bgp_dcid}:34::4 peer-group RS
        address-family ipv4 unicast
          redistribute static
          neighbor RS activate
        exit-address-family

      route-map primary permit 10
        set community 65000:1
      route-map secondary permit 10
        set community 65000:2

      ipv6 nht resolve-via-default

  # Linode's own Network Helper reflects every buddy IP-failover address
  # this node is currently backing up onto eth0 AS A /24 (e.g.
  # Address=172.236.171.170/24), not a /32 -- creating an on-link subnet
  # route (172.236.171.0/24 dev eth0 src 172.236.171.170) that's MORE
  # SPECIFIC than, and so wins over, the default route for anything else
  # in that same /24 -- including, if Linode's own DNS resolvers happen
  # to fall in it, this node's own outbound DNS queries (breaking
  # natctl's own discover() calls to api.linode.com with a DNS
  # NameResolutionError, leaving this node's roster permanently empty).
  # Explicit /32 host routes for each DNS server,
  # via the default gateway with this node's OWN address as source,
  # override the wrongly-preferred on-link route for exactly those
  # destinations without touching eth0's own addressing (which Network
  # Helper must stay free to keep reflecting the fleet's live failover
  # state). Unlike the one-time PUB_IP runcmd steps below, buddy
  # addresses (and so this exact route conflict) can appear or change
  # on ANY later reboot, not just first boot -- cloud-init's own runcmd
  # never re-runs after first boot, so this has to be a real systemd
  # unit that re-applies itself on every boot, not another runcmd line.
  - path: /usr/local/bin/lng-dns-route-fixup.sh
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0750'
    content: |
      #!/bin/sh
      set -eu
%{ if reserved_public_ip != "" ~}
      PUB_IP="${reserved_public_ip}"
%{ else ~}
      PUB_IP=$(ip -4 -o addr show eth0 | awk '{print $4}' | cut -d/ -f1 | head -n1)
%{ endif ~}
      GW=$(ip -4 route show default dev eth0 | awk '{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) if ($i=="via") print $(i+1)}' | head -n1)
      for dns_ip in $(grep '^DNS=' /etc/systemd/network/05-eth0.network 2>/dev/null | head -n1 | cut -d= -f2); do
        ip route replace "$dns_ip/32" via "$GW" src "$PUB_IP" dev eth0
      done

  - path: /etc/systemd/system/lng-dns-route-fixup.service
    owner: root:root
    permissions: '0644'
    content: |
      [Unit]
      Description=LNG: pin host routes for Linode's DNS resolvers to this node's own address
      After=systemd-networkd.service network-online.target
      Wants=network-online.target

      [Service]
      Type=oneshot
      ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/lng-dns-route-fixup.sh

      [Install]
      WantedBy=multi-user.target
%{ endif ~}

runcmd:
  - sysctl --system
  # Linode's own boot-time "Network Helper" (its
  # /etc/systemd/network/05-eth*.network files, regenerated on every boot,
  # outside this project's control) statically assigns the SAME DNS
  # resolver IPs to eth0 (public, DNS actually reachable) AND eth1 (VPC,
  # deliberately isolated -- §3.0/§3.7 -- DNS is NOT reachable there).
  # systemd-resolved then treats eth1 as a valid DNS scope alongside eth0,
  # and a query that happens to race onto the eth1 path silently hangs
  # until timeout instead of failing over -- this reproducibly causes
  # exporter.py's own `curl https://...` egress_reachable probe (which
  # needs to resolve a hostname) to intermittently time out and report a
  # fully-functional node as unhealthy. These two
  # lower-numbered (04-, processed before Network Helper's own 05-) files
  # fully take over eth1/eth2's config each boot, republishing the same
  # DHCP=no/Address= Network Helper already sets but omitting the DNS=
  # line entirely -- so systemd-resolved never sees a scope for either
  # link, and every DNS query goes out eth0 only, where it's actually
  # answerable. IPv4 forwarding/NAT itself is never affected by this --
  # only the health check's own DNS-dependent methodology is.
  - mkdir -p /etc/systemd/network
  # This instance's VPC interface only ever gets a
  # kernel route for its OWN directly-connected subnet -- nothing (not
  # Linode's Network Helper, not this project without these explicit
  # routes) routes it to any
  # OTHER subnet in the same VPC, so a packet to a sibling subnet (e.g. a
  # customer's client instance on a different VPC subnet) would fall through to
  # the default route (out the PUBLIC interface, eth0) and be silently
  # dropped there -- even though Cloud Firewall's private_subnet_ids
  # rule (terraform/modules/vpc/main.tf) already allows it. vpc_sibling_subnet_cidrs
  # is Terraform-auto-discovered (every subnet in the VPC, via
  # terraform/modules/vpc's linode_vpc_subnets data source), not
  # hand-maintained -- purely a routing convenience, not a new security
  # boundary: Cloud Firewall/nftables remain the only thing that decides
  # what's actually reachable.
  - |
    printf '[Match]\nName=eth1\n\n[Network]\nDHCP=no\nAddress=${vpc_ip}/${vpc_prefix}\nIPv6PrivacyExtensions=false\n%{ for cidr in vpc_sibling_subnet_cidrs }\n[Route]\nDestination=${cidr}\n%{ endfor }' > /etc/systemd/network/04-lng-eth1-no-dns.network
  - |
    printf '[Match]\nName=eth2\n\n[Network]\nDHCP=no\nAddress=${vlan_ip}/${vlan_prefix}\nIPv6PrivacyExtensions=false\n' > /etc/systemd/network/04-lng-eth2-no-dns.network
  - systemctl restart systemd-networkd
  # nftables.conf is fetched from Object Storage (per-node object,
  # uploaded by terraform/modules/nat-fleet -- differs per node by IP/CIDR,
  # so it isn't a candidate for the shared, one-per-environment artifacts
  # module), not embedded -- see this file's header note above.
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${nftables_conf_url}" -o /etc/nftables.conf
  - chown root:root /etc/nftables.conf
  - chmod 0640 /etc/nftables.conf
  - systemctl enable --now nftables
  - nft -f /etc/nftables.conf
  # Self-discover this node's own public IP(s) now that Linode has assigned
  # them, and hand that to the exporter — see the note on /etc/nat-exporter/env
  # above for why this can't be a template variable.
  - |
    PUB_IPS=$(ip -4 -o addr show eth0 | awk '{print $4}' | cut -d/ -f1 | paste -sd, -)
    echo "NAT_PUBLIC_IPS=$${PUB_IPS}" >> /etc/nat-exporter/env
  # exporter.py is fetched from Object Storage (terraform/modules/
  # artifacts), not embedded in this document -- see that module's main.tf
  # header comment (Linode's 16384-byte decoded cloud-init limit). `curl`
  # is in packages: above, so it's already installed by the time runcmd
  # runs. -f makes curl fail loudly (non-zero exit -> this whole cloud-init
  # run reports failure, visible in `cloud-init status`) rather than
  # silently writing an empty/error-page file if the fetch fails.
  # nat-exporter.service (the unit file itself) is fetched
  # the same way, one more static, non-secret file the shared artifacts
  # module uploads once per environment. agent_distribution picks
  # between the .py source path (run via python3, the default) and a
  # pre-compiled binary path -- see
  # controller/natctl/cloud_init.py's matching comment, this
  # renderer must stay in sync with that one.
  - mkdir -p /opt/nat-exporter
%{ if agent_distribution == "binary" ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${exporter_bin_url}" -o /opt/nat-exporter/nat-exporter
  - chown root:root /opt/nat-exporter/nat-exporter
  - chmod 0750 /opt/nat-exporter/nat-exporter
%{ else ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${exporter_py_url}" -o /opt/nat-exporter/exporter.py
  - chown root:root /opt/nat-exporter/exporter.py
  - chmod 0750 /opt/nat-exporter/exporter.py
%{ endif ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${nat_exporter_service_url}" -o /etc/systemd/system/nat-exporter.service
  - chown root:root /etc/systemd/system/nat-exporter.service
  - chmod 0644 /etc/systemd/system/nat-exporter.service
  - systemctl daemon-reload
  # nat-exporter is deliberately NOT started here -- its own
  # `systemctl enable --now nat-exporter` runs later in this file (see
  # below), after the ip_failover_enabled block has had a chance to
  # write its own NAT_PUBLIC_IP line to /etc/nat-exporter/env.
  # nat-exporter's systemd unit reads that env file only once at process
  # start, and cloud-init's runcmd never re-runs on a later reboot, so
  # starting it here would mean the running process never picks up that
  # value at all. It must start after BOTH NAT_PUBLIC_IPS (written
  # above) and NAT_PUBLIC_IP (written conditionally, below) exist.
%{ if ip_failover_enabled ~}
  # Bring up FRR and push this node's own public IP in as router-id +
  # the "primary" self-announcement — self-detected (not known until
  # Linode creates this instance, same constraint as NAT_PUBLIC_IPS
  # above), applied exactly once, and never touched again. Also appends
  # NAT_PUBLIC_IP to buddy-sync's env file, which MUST happen before
  # lng-buddy-sync starts below (it reads that file once at process
  # start). buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py only ever touches the *secondary*
  # network statement afterward, deliberately: a
  # `bgp router-id` change forces a full BGP session reset on every peer,
  # while a `network ... route-map` statement change does not.
  # write_files left frr.conf as root:root (see that entry's comment
  # above for why) -- fix ownership here, now that the frr package (and
  # its frr:frr system user) is guaranteed present, before frr starts.
  - chown frr:frr /etc/frr/frr.conf
  - systemctl enable --now frr
  # --now runs it immediately (fixing this exact boot); `enable` is what
  # actually matters for a later reboot, once a buddy IP-failover address
  # already exists on eth0 before this unit gets a chance to run -- see
  # the write_files entries above for the full write-up.
  - systemctl daemon-reload
  - systemctl enable --now lng-dns-route-fixup.service
  - |
%{ if reserved_public_ip != "" ~}
    # reserved_ip_enabled: this node's eth0 address is a Reserved IP,
    # already known at Terraform-render time (see nat-fleet/main.tf and
    # nftables.conf.tftpl's matching comment) -- no need to self-detect it
    # off eth0 here, which matters once a Reserved IP is in play since
    # `ip addr show eth0 | head -n1` has no way to guarantee it picks the
    # reserved address specifically over any other address that might be
    # present on the interface.
    PUB_IP="${reserved_public_ip}"
%{ else ~}
    PUB_IP=$(ip -4 -o addr show eth0 | awk '{print $4}' | cut -d/ -f1 | head -n1)
%{ endif ~}
    mkdir -p /etc/systemd/network
    printf '[Match]\nName=lo\n\n[Network]\nAddress=%s/32\n' "$PUB_IP" > /etc/systemd/network/10-lng-ip-failover.network
    systemctl restart systemd-networkd
    vtysh -c "configure terminal" -c "router bgp 65001" -c "bgp router-id $PUB_IP" -c "address-family ipv4 unicast" -c "network $PUB_IP/32 route-map primary" -c "exit-address-family" -c "end" -c "write memory"
    echo "NAT_PUBLIC_IP=$${PUB_IP}" >> /etc/lng-buddy-sync/env
    # Same unambiguous self-detected PUB_IP, also given to the exporter
    # so its egress_reachable probe can pin its source address instead
    # of picking ambiguously among eth0's addresses once a buddy IP is
    # also present there -- see exporter.py's own comment.
    echo "NAT_PUBLIC_IP=$${PUB_IP}" >> /etc/nat-exporter/env
%{ endif ~}
  - systemctl enable --now nat-exporter
%{ if natctl_roster_url != "" ~}
  # `systemctl mask` (not just disable) the conntrack-tools package's own
  # default conntrackd.service -- apt installing `conntrackd` auto-enables
  # it with an empty stub /etc/conntrackd/conntrackd.conf, which would
  # otherwise be free to bind UDP port 3780 and collide with one of
  # buddy_sync.py's own per-peer conntrackd@<peer_node_id> instances
  # (see apply_conntrack_peers() in buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py). Masking also
  # blocks anything else on the box from starting it later -- every
  # conntrackd process this node ever runs goes through a
  # conntrackd@<peer_node_id> instance from here on, never the bare
  # default unit. Those per-peer instances themselves are intentionally
  # NOT enabled here — buddy_sync.py starts each one once it learns that
  # real peer from natctl, so nothing ever runs with an empty/bogus
  # config in the window before that.
  - systemctl mask --now conntrackd
  # buddy_sync.py is fetched from Object Storage, not embedded -- see
  # the exporter.py comment above for the same reasoning/flags.
  # lng-buddy-sync.service and the conntrackd@.service TEMPLATE unit
  # (both static, non-secret) are also fetched, not embedded --
  # daemon-reload is required here since systemd hasn't seen either unit
  # file until this curl step runs. See the exporter.py block above's
  # matching comment for the binary-vs-source distinction.
  - mkdir -p /opt/lng-buddy-sync
%{ if agent_distribution == "binary" ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${buddy_sync_bin_url}" -o /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy-sync
  - chown root:root /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy-sync
  - chmod 0750 /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy-sync
%{ else ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${buddy_sync_py_url}" -o /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py
  - chown root:root /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py
  - chmod 0750 /opt/lng-buddy-sync/buddy_sync.py
%{ endif ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${lng_buddy_sync_service_url}" -o /etc/systemd/system/lng-buddy-sync.service
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${conntrackd_peer_service_url}" -o /etc/systemd/system/conntrackd@.service
  - chown root:root /etc/systemd/system/lng-buddy-sync.service /etc/systemd/system/conntrackd@.service
  - chmod 0644 /etc/systemd/system/lng-buddy-sync.service /etc/systemd/system/conntrackd@.service
  - systemctl daemon-reload
  - systemctl enable --now lng-buddy-sync
%{ endif ~}
%{ if natctl_on_node_enabled ~}
  # natctl-on-node. NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID can't be a template variable
  # for the same reason NAT_PUBLIC_IPS/router-id above can't be — this
  # node's own Linode instance ID is only assigned by Linode's API AFTER
  # creation, and this cloud-init payload is embedded in that SAME create
  # call (see the dependency-cycle note at the top of main.tf). Resolved
  # instead via Linode's Instance Metadata Service (IMDS), the same
  # self-lookup mechanism Linode documents for "what is my own instance
  # ID" — this is what lets natctl-on-node's own leader-election fencing
  # (see docs/NAT-GATEWAY-DEFINITIVE-GUIDE.html §2.4 "Leader Election and
  # Fencing") identify and power off a previous leader by Linode ID
  # without needing that value handed in externally. Falls back to
  # leaving NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID unset (rather than writing a bogus
  # value) if IMDS is unreachable or the image doesn't support it; natctl
  # itself refuses to start with leader_election.enabled and no self
  # identity (see main.py's build_leader_election()), so this fails
  # closed, not silently.
  # Parses the IMDS response as plain text, NOT JSON -- Linode's IMDS
  # (http://169.254.169.254/v1/instance) does not return JSON, despite
  # looking like it might: Content-Type: text/plain, body is plain
  # "key: value" lines (id: 102018660, label: ..., etc). Piping this
  # through a JSON parser would raise on every single call, silently
  # leaving NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID unset. Parsing the real plain-text
  # format directly also avoids a python3 dependency for this one line.
  - |
    IMDS_TOKEN=$(curl -s -X PUT -H "Metadata-Token-Expiry-Seconds: 3600" http://169.254.169.254/v1/token)
    if [ -n "$${IMDS_TOKEN}" ]; then
      SELF_LINODE_ID=$(curl -s -H "Metadata-Token: $${IMDS_TOKEN}" http://169.254.169.254/v1/instance | grep '^id: ' | cut -d' ' -f2)
      if [ -n "$${SELF_LINODE_ID}" ]; then
        echo "NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID=$${SELF_LINODE_ID}" >> /etc/natctl/env
      else
        logger -t lng-cloud-init "WARNING: could not parse this node's own Linode instance ID from IMDS -- NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID left unset, natctl will refuse to start if leader_election.enabled"
      fi
    else
      logger -t lng-cloud-init "WARNING: Linode Instance Metadata Service unreachable -- NATCTL_SELF_LINODE_ID left unset, natctl will refuse to start if leader_election.enabled"
    fi
  # The natctl package itself (controller/natctl/*.py) is fetched from
  # Object Storage, one curl per file, not embedded -- see the exporter.py
  # comment above for the same reasoning. One `- curl ... -o ...` line per
  # file (natctl_file_urls, from terraform/modules/artifacts) -- this loop
  # unrolls at Terraform render time into one runcmd entry per file.
  # requirements.txt and the natctl.service unit (both static,
  # non-secret) are also fetched, not embedded.
  # agent_distribution == "binary" replaces the whole per-.py-file loop +
  # pip install with one curl of a single pre-compiled executable -- see
  # controller/natctl/cloud_init.py's matching comment.
%{ if agent_distribution == "binary" ~}
  - mkdir -p /opt/natctl
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${natctl_bin_url}" -o /opt/natctl/natctl
  - chown root:root /opt/natctl/natctl
  - chmod 0750 /opt/natctl/natctl
%{ else ~}
  - mkdir -p /opt/natctl/natctl
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${natctl_requirements_txt_url}" -o /opt/natctl/requirements.txt
%{ for rel_path, url in natctl_file_urls ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${url}" -o /opt/natctl/natctl/${rel_path}
%{ endfor ~}
  - chmod -R 0644 /opt/natctl/natctl
%{ endif ~}
  - curl -fsSL --retry 8 --retry-all-errors --retry-delay 2 "${natctl_service_url}" -o /etc/systemd/system/natctl.service
  - chown root:root /etc/systemd/system/natctl.service
  - chmod 0644 /etc/systemd/system/natctl.service
%{ if agent_distribution != "binary" ~}
  - pip3 install -r /opt/natctl/requirements.txt
%{ endif ~}
  - systemctl daemon-reload
  - systemctl enable --now natctl
%{ endif ~}
  - logger -t lng-cloud-init "LNG NAT node ${node_name} (pool=${pool_name}) bootstrap complete"
